
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Section 1 approved. Section 2 of 3 — [1merror handling and the DOM contract.[0m

  This is where the current markup doesn't cooperate, so it's the section with real decisions in it.

  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m's inputs have [38;5;153mid[39m attributes but no [38;5;153mname[39m attributes. [38;5;153mFormData[39m keys off [38;5;153mname[39m, so the login inputs need
  [38;5;153mname="username"[39m and [38;5;153mname="password"[39m added. That also makes the schema key on [38;5;153mname[39m, which is the same string as the id
  today — no ambiguity, but worth stating because the signup form must follow the same rule.

  There's also nowhere to put an error message. The options are for [38;5;153mbindForm[39m to create error elements on demand, or for
  the markup to declare them. I'm proposing the markup declares them: each field gets a sibling [38;5;153m<span class="error" [39m
  [38;5;153mdata-error-for="username"></span>[39m. The helper finds them by [38;5;153mdata-error-for[39m and sets [38;5;153mtextContent[39m. Reason: a helper that
  injects DOM decides your markup and styling for you and is fiddly to test, while a declared element is visible in the
  HTML, stylable, and trivial to assert against. The cost is two lines of markup per field, and a missing error element
  has to be handled.

  Error handling specifics:

  - [1mMissing error element[0m — [38;5;153mbindForm[39m logs a warning naming the field and continues validating. A markup omission
    shouldn't break form submission, but it also shouldn't fail silently.
  - [1mSchema names a field not in the form[0m — throw at [38;5;153mbindForm[39m time, not at submit time. That's a programmer error and it
    should surface on page load, not on the user's first submit.
  - [1mClearing[0m — every error element is cleared at the start of each submit, so fixed fields stop showing stale messages.
  - [1mAccessibility[0m — error spans get [38;5;153mrole="alert"[39m, and invalid inputs get [38;5;153maria-invalid="true"[39m (removed when they pass).
    Cheap now, and it's the kind of thing that never gets retrofitted.
  - [1m[38;5;153monValid[39m throwing[0m — not caught. Swallowing an application error inside the validation layer would hide real bugs;
    that's the form's own concern.

  One judgment call worth your veto: errors render only on submit, per your earlier choice. So a user fixing a bad email
  sees the message persist until they submit again. Adding on-blur revalidation later is additive — it doesn't change
  any of the above.

  Does section 2 look right?

